WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.277 --> 00:00:01.610
- Today we're going to be talking about

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:04.124
the single greatest time management tip

00:00:04.124 --> 00:00:06.684
I ever received about law school.

00:00:06.684 --> 00:00:08.842
Outlined from day one.

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:11.255
So stick around and get
an edge in law school.

00:00:11.255 --> 00:00:15.439
(melodious instrumental music)

00:00:15.439 --> 00:00:17.734
It's hard to overstate just how important

00:00:17.734 --> 00:00:19.534
your outline is in law school.

00:00:19.534 --> 00:00:22.107
It's going to be your
primary studying device.

00:00:22.107 --> 00:00:25.160
It's how you distill all of
your notes and your cases

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:28.619
and your commercial outlines
into a usable format,

00:00:28.619 --> 00:00:31.858
specifically tailored just for you.

00:00:31.858 --> 00:00:34.487
But the kicker is that
the process of distilling

00:00:34.487 --> 00:00:36.722
everything takes time,

00:00:36.722 --> 00:00:39.975
and it takes way longer than you think.

00:00:39.975 --> 00:00:44.195
And time is the single, greatest
equalizer in law school.

00:00:44.195 --> 00:00:46.170
Everyone has the same amount.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:47.559
And the students who get A's

00:00:47.559 --> 00:00:50.242
are the ones that focus
on what's important

00:00:50.242 --> 00:00:52.792
and they cut out the time wasters.

00:00:52.792 --> 00:00:54.423
In other words, they focus on the stuff

00:00:54.423 --> 00:00:57.273
that actually helps on the final exam.

00:00:57.273 --> 00:00:59.563
Now a good outline is necessary

00:00:59.563 --> 00:01:02.047
but it's not sufficient
to crush your final.

00:01:02.047 --> 00:01:04.486
The point of an outline
is to study from it,

00:01:04.486 --> 00:01:06.806
to organize things in a logical way

00:01:06.806 --> 00:01:08.385
so that you can study the crap

00:01:08.385 --> 00:01:10.586
out of the things that
are in your outline.

00:01:10.586 --> 00:01:14.086
It's a means to an end, not an end itself.

00:01:15.279 --> 00:01:18.653
Now this might seem like an
obvious point but I mention it

00:01:18.653 --> 00:01:21.783
because more than half
of my law school friends

00:01:21.783 --> 00:01:23.441
believe that it was better to wait

00:01:23.441 --> 00:01:26.475
until the end of a semester
to start their outline,

00:01:26.475 --> 00:01:28.865
because they felt that they've studied

00:01:28.865 --> 00:01:31.958
by making the outline itself.

00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:35.502
They thought that the simple
act of making their outline

00:01:35.502 --> 00:01:39.085
was enough to study for their exam.

00:01:39.085 --> 00:01:40.835
But this is so wrong.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:45.356
Now sure the building
of your outline helps,

00:01:45.356 --> 00:01:47.320
but it's only the first step.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.084
Imagine if you were
preparing for a chess match

00:01:50.084 --> 00:01:52.861
by reading books about
the history of chess

00:01:52.861 --> 00:01:55.541
and you waited until the
week before the match

00:01:55.541 --> 00:01:59.119
to actually memorize how
the chess pieces move.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:02.237
It would be much better to
have memorized that stuff

00:02:02.237 --> 00:02:05.609
way ahead of time and
practice before your match,

00:02:05.609 --> 00:02:07.523
and the same in law school.

00:02:07.523 --> 00:02:09.408
Better to have your outline done

00:02:09.408 --> 00:02:11.204
months before your final exam

00:02:11.204 --> 00:02:13.445
instead of wasting your
precious study time

00:02:13.445 --> 00:02:15.356
compiling your outline.

00:02:15.356 --> 00:02:17.544
Then as finals approached,

00:02:17.544 --> 00:02:20.632
you can practice by
applying the information

00:02:20.632 --> 00:02:23.650
that you already put in your outline.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:26.368
What most students don't
understand about their outline

00:02:26.368 --> 00:02:28.618
is that more is not better.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:32.098
There's an old phrase
that, "If I had more time,

00:02:32.098 --> 00:02:34.232
"I would have written a shorter letter."

00:02:34.232 --> 00:02:36.353
The same is true for outlines.

00:02:36.353 --> 00:02:40.258
It takes time to distill
all of that information

00:02:40.258 --> 00:02:41.847
for a particular class

00:02:41.847 --> 00:02:45.097
and to get it into a short usable form.

00:02:45.985 --> 00:02:47.492
Ideally, believe it or not,

00:02:47.492 --> 00:02:51.556
I would say the best outline
is less than 20 pages.

00:02:51.556 --> 00:02:53.954
Some people try to fit
everything their professor

00:02:53.954 --> 00:02:57.373
has ever said in a class
into their outline.

00:02:57.373 --> 00:03:00.909
But this is the exact opposite
of what you should do.

00:03:00.909 --> 00:03:04.763
The longer your outline is,
the harder it is to use.

00:03:04.763 --> 00:03:08.659
And law school exams are
all about the application.

00:03:08.659 --> 00:03:11.014
Your outline is your tool bag.

00:03:11.014 --> 00:03:13.243
You want it organized, you want it clean,

00:03:13.243 --> 00:03:16.206
and you only want the tools
that are going to help you

00:03:16.206 --> 00:03:18.858
for the job at hand in your tool bag.

00:03:18.858 --> 00:03:22.271
For example, a good craftsman
could probably build a table

00:03:22.271 --> 00:03:24.026
with just three tools.

00:03:24.026 --> 00:03:26.138
Sure he might be able to make it faster

00:03:26.138 --> 00:03:28.416
or better with more tools,

00:03:28.416 --> 00:03:30.332
but it's better to have three tools

00:03:30.332 --> 00:03:32.530
that you know like the back of your hand

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:34.924
than to have a hundred
tools that you don't.

00:03:34.924 --> 00:03:36.989
And it's the same way
with law school outlines.

00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:39.999
Much better to know a
smaller outline intimately

00:03:39.999 --> 00:03:43.209
than a longer outline only superficially.

00:03:43.209 --> 00:03:47.221
But distilling all of that
information takes time.

00:03:47.221 --> 00:03:49.740
And I'm not going to lie, it's hard work.

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:52.666
Make no mistake, distilling
all of the information

00:03:52.666 --> 00:03:56.446
from your class, in commercial
outlines and from your peers,

00:03:56.446 --> 00:03:58.934
into a short concise summary

00:03:58.934 --> 00:04:01.740
is one of the hardest
things about law school,

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.734
but it's also invaluable.

00:04:03.734 --> 00:04:07.634
So do yourself a favor
and start from week one.

00:04:07.634 --> 00:04:09.894
The most common objection I hear is,

00:04:09.894 --> 00:04:13.499
"But I can't start my outline
because I need context.

00:04:13.499 --> 00:04:14.930
"I need to wait until I see

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:16.728
"how the whole class fits together

00:04:16.728 --> 00:04:19.743
"before I start distilling things down."

00:04:19.743 --> 00:04:21.785
Well I have bad news for you.

00:04:21.785 --> 00:04:25.528
You will never have the
whole context for a class.

00:04:25.528 --> 00:04:28.258
And you'll always have
an excuse to put it off.

00:04:28.258 --> 00:04:31.712
But you have to act and
just start doing it.

00:04:31.712 --> 00:04:34.592
Nassim Taleb, author of
The Black Swan wrote.

00:04:34.592 --> 00:04:36.270
"Understanding how to act

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.905
"under conditions of
incomplete information

00:04:38.905 --> 00:04:42.667
"is the highest and most
urgent human pursuit."

00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:44.408
Amen to that.

00:04:44.408 --> 00:04:48.180
Make your outline starting
week one and then revised it.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:49.887
You can always go back,

00:04:49.887 --> 00:04:52.458
and you'll probably find that
very little of what you write

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:55.793
during the first week of class
will be used on the final.

00:04:55.793 --> 00:04:57.793
You can always go back and revise it.

00:04:57.793 --> 00:05:00.958
You'll understand more as
the semester progresses.

00:05:00.958 --> 00:05:04.937
But it is far easier to revise as you go

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:08.472
than it is to start from scratch
just a week before finals.

00:05:08.472 --> 00:05:10.229
Do what the startups do.

00:05:10.229 --> 00:05:13.933
Build a beta version and
then iterate from there.

00:05:13.933 --> 00:05:15.314
Here's what I suggest.

00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:17.829
Monday through Friday
during a regular semester,

00:05:17.829 --> 00:05:20.797
consider it your job to
listen to the professor,

00:05:20.797 --> 00:05:23.234
take good notes, read your
commercial supplements,

00:05:23.234 --> 00:05:26.530
and try and understand as much
as you can about the class.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:28.734
And then over the weekend,

00:05:28.734 --> 00:05:31.997
distill all of that
information into an outline.

00:05:31.997 --> 00:05:35.414
No more than one page per class per week.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:40.133
If you can do that, by the
time the finals come around,

00:05:40.133 --> 00:05:41.678
you'll be done with your outline

00:05:41.678 --> 00:05:45.101
and you'll already be
crushing your practice exams.

00:05:45.101 --> 00:05:48.295
It just takes a little work every week.

00:05:48.295 --> 00:05:50.858
It's a marathon not a sprint.

00:05:50.858 --> 00:05:55.316
So start early, be consistent,
and you'll crush finals

00:05:55.316 --> 00:05:58.196
with a kick ass outline
that you started writing

00:05:58.196 --> 00:06:01.694
back in the first week of the semester.

